From 69cc20045c913892f8a5d1a8dd5c23671dc3af71 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Sat, 7 Nov 2026 17:56:52 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] yuzu_cmd: run eden-cli with Eden Qt's config for the game

eden-cli read only its own sdl2-config.ini, so a game launched from it
ran with its defaults (keyboard controls, stock graphics) instead of the
settings tuned in Eden Qt.

When <config dir>/qt-config.ini exists and no -c names a config,
eden-cli now saves its own config first (so no Qt value lands in it),
then reads qt-config.ini as a global config and the game's
custom/<program id>.ini as a per-game config into Settings, through the
shared Config readers and SdlConfig's player reader: every section
frontend_common reads, the player mappings, debug pad, ring controller,
and the two [UI] values the core uses, external_content_dirs (under
[UI] as Paths\external_content_dirs) and the screenshot dir (under [UI]
as Screenshots\screenshot_path), read as QtConfig::ReadUIValues does,
with each per-game value applied by its use_global flag as Eden Qt's
BootGame does. Qt's DisabledAddOns list replaces sdl2-config.ini's
instead of merging with it. The launch params are applied after both
files, so a command-line flag always wins. The reader never gets a
file path, so no save path can write the Qt files, and an absent, empty
or unreadable file, or one without the [Controls] section Eden Qt always
writes, leaves Settings as they were.
One log line names what was applied, e.g.
"Duo config: qt-config.ini + custom/0100F2C0115B6000.ini", or
"Duo config: eden-cli's own config only (-c given)".

The program id needs a loader, so the content provider, filesystem and
factories are set up before System::Initialize instead of after the
window; the per-game values are then in place for Initialize and the
renderer pick. After the Qt reads the factories are rebuilt, as Eden
Qt does after a config change, so the NAND, SD, mod, dump and external
content dirs are Eden Qt's (CreateFactories reads them once; the call
in Services does not overwrite). Without qt-config.ini, or with -c,
eden-cli behaves as before apart from that order. The Qt reads, the log
line, the factory rebuild, the profile re-read, -u and the launch params
run in one Duo hook,
DSModHarness::ApplyEdenQtConfig; yuzu.cpp keeps the moved filesystem
lines and that call. .upstream/DIVERGENCES.md row 30 records both for
upstream syncs.

The account list was read once, from the default NAND with user 0,
before any config was read. As Eden Qt's FixProfiles does after its
config load, eden-cli now calls ResetUserSaveFile after the Qt config
reads and reopens the -u user after the launch params, so the save and
the companion's save reader use the account and NAND Eden Qt uses. -u
<name> is resolved after the config reads. With eden-cli's own config
the accounts load as before.

-c writes its file back, so a -c that resolves (absolute, "..",
symlinks; case-folded on Windows) to a file named qt-config.ini or one
directly in a dir named custom, in any dir (portable mode moves
eden-cli's config dir but not Eden Qt's), or that does not resolve, is
refused with one error line. A hard link to a Qt file under another
name is not caught. The check sits in the option parser, so --install
and --dump-il2cpp are covered too.

Known limits, each a shortcut comment: program id 0 (homebrew) gets no
per-game file (Eden Qt names that one after the file); Qt keyboard
bindings do not carry over: Eden Qt saves every one (default or
custom) as \default=false plus its Qt::Key code, which eden-cli takes
as an SDL scancode, so each lands on another key (controller bindings
match); updates and DLC that Eden Qt registers from its game dirs
(ext_content_from_game_dirs) are not loaded, only those in the NAND and
the external content dirs; qt-config.ini is looked up only in
eden-cli's own config dir, so a portable Eden Qt or eden-cli does not
see the other's files; a qt-config.ini that Eden Qt is rewriting at that
moment (WriteToIni truncates first) can be read half-written, so close
Eden Qt before starting eden-cli.

Test (vibe-window host/eden-duo/test/qt_config_test.cpp, natively and
under wine): own config, qt-config.ini, then custom/<id>.ini; use_global
true and false; a per-game input profile; Qt files byte- and
mtime-identical, nothing created, no save attempted; absent, empty and
garbage files refused; external_content_dirs and the screenshot dir
read from [UI]; a stale DisabledAddOns entry dropped; -c refused for
qt-config.ini and custom/ files in eden-cli's config dir and elsewhere,
"..", and (Windows) case and slash variants, allowed for
sdl2-config.ini.

